menuconfig OBJZ
	bool "Objective-Z transpiler support"
	depends on CPU_CORTEX_M || CPU_CORTEX_A || (RISCV && !RISCV_ISA_RV32E)
	select STATIC_INIT_GNU
	help
	  Enable Objective-Z transpiled C support with the Platform
	  Abstraction Layer (PAL). The transpiler converts Objective-C
	  (.m) sources to plain C at build time via Clang AST analysis.

	  Collections need one option this deliberately does not turn on:
	  CONFIG_SYS_MEM_BLOCKS=y. OZArray, OZDictionary and the @[...]
	  and @{...} literals draw their element buffers from Zephyr's
	  contiguous block allocator, which is behind that option and
	  defaults to n. It stays opt-in because whether a program uses a
	  collection is a property of its Objective-C, not of its
	  configuration -- most programs use none, and a select here would
	  take away their ability to say n. A program that does need it
	  and has not set it stops at the item pool's definition with a
	  BUILD_ASSERT naming the option, rather than at the link with
	  undefined references into the PAL (#366).

	  Leaving it on costs a program that uses no collection nothing:
	  samples/hello_category is 26808 B flash and 6852 B RAM on
	  mps2/an385 either way, because --gc-sections drops the whole
	  library when nothing calls it.

if OBJZ

# The transpiler. There used to be a choice here between the Rust pipeline
# and a Python one (oz_transpile); the Python backend is retired, so
# CONFIG_OBJZ_BACKEND_STATIC is unconditional and CONFIG_OBJZ_BACKEND_PYTHON
# no longer exists. A build that still sets it will fail Kconfig's unknown
# symbol check rather than silently selecting something else.
#
# The implementation is readable at the `python-backend-final` tag.
config OBJZ_BACKEND_STATIC
	bool
	default y
	help
	  The transpiler: .m -> tree-sitter CST -> oz2c -> .h + .c. Rejects
	  anything outside its supported subset with a located error rather
	  than emitting code that misbehaves.

config OBJZ_INTROSPECTION
	bool "Type introspection (-isKindOfClass:, -conformsToProtocol:)"
	default y
	help
	  Answer -isKindOfClass: and -conformsToProtocol: from tables
	  generated at transpile time -- a superclass chain indexed by
	  class id, and one conformance bitmap per protocol. Both are
	  const, so they cost flash and no RAM, and neither is emitted
	  unless some call site actually uses it: a program that does not
	  introspect pays nothing for leaving this on.

	  Class identity is not covered here and is always available:
	  [Foo class] is a compile-time constant, and [obj class] and
	  -isMemberOfClass: read the class id every object already carries.

	  Set n to forbid type introspection outright -- for code-size or
	  certification discipline. Both selectors then become located
	  transpile errors naming this option, never silently unavailable.

config OBJZ_REFLECTION
	bool "Selectors (@selector, SEL, -respondsToSelector:, -performSelector:)"
	default y
	help
	  Make @selector(...) and SEL real values, and answer
	  -respondsToSelector: and the -performSelector: family from them.

	  A SEL is a pointer to a const record per selector some
	  @selector(...) names: a responds bitmap keyed by class id, and a
	  uniform-shape wrapper so an indirect call needs no cast and no
	  variadics. Costs more than introspection when used -- roughly 16
	  bytes of flash per selector plus the dispatch function it forces
	  into existence for a selector that had only one implementor -- and
	  still nothing at all for a program that names no selector.

	  Because a SEL is a value, there is no way to prove which selector
	  reaches which -performSelector: call site. So in a program that
	  performs, every selector named by a @selector(...) must be
	  performable: at most two arguments, all object-typed, returning
	  void or an object. One that is not is a located transpile error
	  rather than a runtime surprise.

	  Set n to forbid selectors outright. All four constructs then
	  become located transpile errors naming this option.

config OBJZ_DEBUG_LINES
	bool "#line directives back to the Objective-C source"
	depends on DEBUG
	default y
	help
	  Put #line directives on the generated C, so the toolchain
	  resolves a transpiled program's addresses to the .m the code was
	  written in rather than to oz_static_generated/<Class>.c, the only
	  file the C compiler is ever handed.

	  With this on, break PXLEDController.m:161, list, step and info
	  line work in Objective-C terms; a fatal-error backtrace,
	  addr2line and west debug name the .m; a GCC warning about
	  generated code quotes the Objective-C that produced it; and
	  coverage attributes to the .m. Paths are absolute, so a debugger
	  resolves them from any working directory.

	  Free of image, and not free of source. #line changes only what
	  the compiler records about the code, not the code, so every
	  generated .o's instructions are byte-identical with it on. The
	  one thing that moves is __FILE__, which then expands to the .m
	  inside generated code, so an __ASSERT failure names the
	  Objective-C as well -- and the image gets slightly smaller,
	  since a .m name is shorter than a generated .c path: 26848 bytes
	  against 26932 for samples/hello_category on mps2/an385.

	  What it costs is the generated C itself, which is what a
	  maintainer reads when the transpiler is the thing under
	  suspicion. Every directive carries an absolute path, so the text
	  roughly doubles: that sample's main.c goes from 2658 bytes to
	  5766, and #line accounts for 38% of the bytes in px-keyboard's
	  whole generated tree, 31% to 55% file by file. Those figures
	  track how deep the build directory sits, the paths being
	  absolute, so they are a shape rather than a constant.

	  So this depends on CONFIG_DEBUG and is y within it (#395). A
	  debugging build gets .m attribution; a release build gets
	  generated C worth reading, and hands back that ~100 bytes of
	  flash.

	  #358 made only the default follow CONFIG_DEBUG, leaving y
	  reachable by hand in a release build on the grounds that a field
	  fault is what has to be resolved against the .m. That does not
	  survive: reading a release image's addresses in .m terms needs the
	  directives to have been in the build that shipped, and a build
	  that sets CONFIG_DEBUG to get them is at -Og with asserts on --
	  a different image, so the hatch answers a different question than
	  the one it was kept for. What it did leave reachable was y in a
	  configuration where the directives are pure cost.

	  Covered: method bodies, plain C function bodies and the functions
	  hoisted from block literals -- the code you actually step
	  through. Code oz_static synthesizes (slab definitions,
	  _oz_release_ivars, dispatch thunks, introspection tables) keeps
	  pointing at the generated .c, which is where it genuinely lives.

	  Set n to read the generated C directly without line markers in
	  it, or if some tool in the chain mishandles them.

config OBJZ_DEFAULT_DESCRIPTION
	bool "Default -cDescription:maxLength: naming the class"
	default y
	help
	  Give every class an inherited description of the form
	  <ClassName: 0xADDRESS>, so OZLog's %@ names an object rather
	  than printing nothing. A class that writes its own
	  -cDescription:maxLength: overrides it either way; this only
	  decides what the classes that do not write one produce.

	  Costs ~360 bytes of flash, and costs them on every program that
	  calls OZLog at all -- not only those using %@. The %@ branch
	  lives in oz_log's body and the format string is a run-time
	  value, so the linker cannot know the branch is unreachable and
	  keeps the whole chain behind it: the protocol dispatch,
	  OZObject's implementation (176 bytes, of which 110 is formatting
	  the address), the synthesized oz_static_class_name (40 bytes) and
	  one name string per class. Measured on mps2/an385:
	  samples/hello_category, which contains no %@ at all, is
	  26820 bytes with this on and 26456 with it off.

	  Set n to get all of it back -- with the call compiled out the
	  linker does strip the rest, and that 26456 is byte for byte what
	  the sample measured before the default existed. The inherited
	  description then writes nothing, and %@ on a class without its
	  own description produces an empty field again, which is what this
	  option was added to fix (#354). That is the trade.

	  Host builds of the test corpora always have this on: it is
	  guarded on CONFIG_OBJZ, which only a Zephyr build defines.

config OBJZ_HEAP
	bool "Enable heap-based allocation (allocWithHeap:)"
	depends on HEAP_MEM_POOL_SIZE > 0
	select SYS_HEAP_RUNTIME_STATS
	help
	  Allow objects to be allocated from an OZHeap instead of
	  k_mem_slab.  Adds +allocWithHeap: class method and
	  heap-aware free path using CONTAINER_OF.
	  Enables SYS_HEAP_RUNTIME_STATS for heap usage queries.

endif # OBJZ
